WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.240
They can litter sidewalks, menace pedestrians
and endanger their riders’ lives.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.889
Over the past year, app-enabled electric scooters
have popped up in cities around the world,

00:00:13.889 --> 00:00:19.810
inviting a wary public to hop a ride as indignant
local governments scramble to regulate them.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.990
It may come as a surprise, then, that e-scooters
could be exactly what traffic-choked cities

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.380
need.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.119
This is your Bloomberg QuickTake on e-scooters.

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:35.860
Download an app, find a scooter, unlock it,
and go for a ride.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.070
When you’re done, leave it behind.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.770
Rides can cost less than $2.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:45.390
There's, Bird, Lime, Scoot,
Skip, both Uber and Lyft have scooters now

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.520
as well.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.600
They're new and by their essence, they're
all over the place.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.740
So if you're not using them, they get in your
way and because people are just learning how

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.850
to use them, they tend to ride them in obnoxious
ways.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.850
And in a lot of places these become sort of
a symbol of the technology industry which

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.180
annoys people.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.540
At launch, cities like Cleveland banned them,
while San Francisco halted operations for

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.229
several months to create a permit system that
capped the number of scooters allowed.

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:18.759
Still, growth has continued: Bird now offers
scooters in about 40 cities, while Lime is

00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:19.759
in 23.

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:21.969
But despite the controversies, e-scooters
have their defenders.

00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:26.310
In dense urban areas, cars often aren’t
the fastest way to get around.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.900
Many cities have turned to bike-share systems
and dedicated bike lanes because they take

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.700
up less space than cars, and save on carbon
emissions.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.450
Some urban planners also see e-scooters as
part of the future of city transportation.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.569
The hope would be that you would eliminate
a lot of shorter car trips.

00:01:41.569 --> 00:01:43.951
That would get a lot of cars off the road
which would allow for some rethinking of the

00:01:43.951 --> 00:01:46.659
streets themselves, and which would create
more protected lanes and so on.

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:49.740
And then, that would in turn broaden the demand
for scooters.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.880
So it would be sort of a virtuous cycle.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.069
But despite Bird and Lime each being valued
at more than $1 billion, it’s too early

00:01:57.069 --> 00:01:59.079
to tell if they will become viable businesses.

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:03.279
the things you look at are, how long
it takes to pay off a vehicle, and how long

00:02:03.279 --> 00:02:05.039
a vehicle stays on the road.

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:09.810
We don't know exactly how much this is, but
you know, it does seem like you can pay these

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.420
things off fairly quickly, they’re relatively
inexpensive.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.830
But, they also just get completely trashed,
very quickly.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:20.950
You're going to take care of your own car,
but, as they say, "no one ever washes a rental

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:21.450
car."

